---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import SkillTrial from '../../../layouts/SkillTrial.astro';
import Figure from '../../../components/coordinate-trial/a00/Figure.astro';
import SpaceLadder from '../../../components/coordinate-trial/a00/SpaceLadder.astro';
import PivotFigure from '../../../components/coordinate-trial/a00/PivotFigure.astro';
import OrderFigure from '../../../components/coordinate-trial/a00/OrderFigure.astro';
import BrowserFigure from '../../../components/coordinate-trial/a00/BrowserFigure.astro';
import {example,spaces} from '../../../components/coordinate-trial/example.mjs';
import {route,pair,cardWorld,cardView,cardClient,collapsedClient} from '../../../components/coordinate-trial/a00/model.mjs';
import {point} from '../../../components/coordinate-trial/geometry.mjs';
import editorUrl from '../../../components/coordinate-trial/a00/editor.html?url&no-inline';
const codeTheme = (name: string, type: 'light' | 'dark') => ({
  name, type,
  colors: {
    'editor.foreground': 'var(--color-text)',
    'editor.background': 'var(--color-code-bg)',
  },
  tokenColors: [
    { scope: ['comment', 'string'], settings: { foreground: 'var(--color-text-secondary)' } },
    { scope: ['keyword', 'storage', 'entity.name.tag', 'support.type'], settings: { foreground: 'var(--color-accent)' } },
    { scope: ['entity.name.function', 'support.function'], settings: { foreground: 'var(--color-accent-strong)' } },
  ],
});
const themes = {
  light: codeTheme('coordinate-light', 'light'),
  dark: codeTheme('coordinate-dark', 'dark'),
};
const nested=`<svg id="editor" viewBox="0 0 800 600">
  <g id="world" transform="matrix(2 0 0 2 60 40)">
    <g id="lane" transform="matrix(0 1 -1 0 160 100)">
      <g id="card" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 30 20)">
        <rect width="80" height="40" />
        <circle cx="10" cy="10" r="3" />
      </g>
    </g>
  </g>
</svg>`;
const matrixCode=`const cardToGroup = new DOMMatrix().translate(30, 20);
const groupToWorld = new DOMMatrix().translate(160, 100).rotate(90);
const worldToView = new DOMMatrix().translate(60, 40).scale(2);
const cardToView = worldToView.multiply(groupToWorld).multiply(cardToGroup);
const pView = new DOMPoint(10, 10).matrixTransform(cardToView);
// pView.x = 320; pView.y = 320`;
const guarded=`function clientToLocal(element, clientX, clientY) {
  if (![clientX, clientY].every(Number.isFinite)) return null;
  const raw = element.getScreenCTM();
  if (!raw || raw.is2D === false) return null;
  const m = DOMMatrix.fromMatrix(raw);
  if (!m.is2D) return null;
  const values = n => [n.a, n.b, n.c, n.d, n.e, n.f];
  if (!values(m).every(Number.isFinite)) return null;
  const undo = m.inverse();
  if (!undo.is2D || !values(undo).every(Number.isFinite)) return null;
  const p = new DOMPoint(clientX, clientY).matrixTransform(undo);
  return [p.x, p.y].every(Number.isFinite) ? p : null;
}

editor.addEventListener('pointerdown', event => {
  const p = clientToLocal(card, event.clientX, event.clientY);
  if (!p) return; // Keep the last valid interaction state.
  const inside = 0 <= p.x && p.x <= 80 && 0 <= p.y && p.y <= 40;
  report(inside, p); // Application-specific selection or feedback.
});`;
const applyCode=`function paint(element, m) {
  element.setAttribute('transform',
    'matrix(' + [m.a, m.b, m.c, m.d, m.e, m.f].join(' ') + ')');
}
paint(world, worldToView);
paint(lane, groupToWorld);
paint(card, cardToGroup);`;
---
<SkillTrial title="Coordinates in a zoomable editor" description="Follow one card through nested placement, rotation and zoom, then map the browser pointer back to its local geometry with a guarded SVG implementation.">
 <p>A diagram editor needs to answer a small question: which spot on a card is under the pointer? The card may sit inside a turned group, and the whole board may be zoomed and moved. Comparing the pointer’s coordinates directly with the card’s rectangle then gives the wrong answer, because those numbers are measured from different origins, along different axes and in different units.</p>
 <p>Keep the card’s geometry in its own coordinates. Describe each placement as a conversion to its parent’s coordinates, combine the conversions to draw it, and undo that combined mapping to interpret input. This article builds that route from ordinary x and y arithmetic, then implements it with SVG and the browser’s <code>DOMMatrix</code> and <code>DOMPoint</code> interfaces. The same route supports pan, zoom, rotation and dragging.</p>
 <p>Every example follows one invented board: a rectangular <strong>Review</strong> card inside <strong>Review lane</strong>. Its marked spot is ten units right and ten units down from its own upper-left corner. The figures’ numerical snapshots are mathematical models. The live editor later uses actual browser coordinates, which depend on where and how large this page draws it.</p>


 <h2 id="spaces">One card, several sets of coordinates</h2>
 <p>A <strong>coordinate space</strong> supplies an origin, axis directions and units. The pair (10, 10) only identifies a position once you say which space it belongs to. In the card’s space, <strong>O</strong>, the origin is its untransformed upper-left corner, and the rectangle extends from x = 0 to 80 and y = 0 to 40. We store that rectangle and its marked spot here.</p>
 <p>To place Review inside Review lane, add (30, 20). The marked spot becomes (40, 30) in the lane’s <strong>group space G</strong>. The board has its own stable <strong>world space W</strong>. Review lane is turned 90° clockwise, then placed at world (160, 100), which puts the marked spot at world (130, 140).</p>
 <p>The editor’s <strong>view space V</strong> is the root SVG’s user space: the coordinates used by its <code>viewBox</code>. Here it spans 800 by 600 units. The view doubles world coordinates and adds pan (60, 40), bringing the spot to V (320, 320). Finally, the browser fits and positions that SVG on the page. Call this conversion from V to browser client coordinates the <strong>browser bridge</strong>. In our worked snapshot it is 800 by 600 CSS pixels with its top-left at (120, 80), so the spot is (440, 400) in <strong>client space C</strong>. Client coordinates are measured in CSS pixels from the browser viewport’s top-left; CSS pixels are layout units, not necessarily individual hardware pixels.</p>
 <SpaceLadder />
 <p>Initially x points right and y points down. A positive angle turns clockwise in that frame. Local axes move with their object: after Review lane turns, the card’s positive x direction points down the page and its positive y direction points left. The spot itself has not changed. Its description has.</p>
 <p>For display, follow O → G → W → V → C. Input arrives in C and needs the reverse route. Changing the view should change that route, while the stored rectangle stays 80 by 40 and its marked spot stays O (10, 10).</p>

 <h2 id="points">A position and a movement follow different rules</h2>
 <p>A <strong>point</strong> is a position relative to an origin. A <strong>displacement</strong> is the change between two positions measured in the same space. Take a second card point q at O (20, 10). Subtract the marked point p at O (10, 10): q − p = (10, 0), a step of ten units along the card’s x-axis. Add that displacement back to p and you recover q.</p>
 <Figure number={2} title="Point and step" caption="The placement adds (30, 20) to both points. Subtraction cancels that translation; the ten-unit step is unchanged. The later group turn changes the step’s direction. Illustrative arithmetic." text="In O, p=(10,10), q=(20,10), q−p=(10,0). In G, p=(40,30), q=(50,30), q−p=(10,0). In W the step is (0,10), and in V it is (0,20).">
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Point and displacement through the placements"><table class="coordinate-data"><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Card O</th><th>Group G</th><th>World W</th><th>View V</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>p</th>{route().slice(0,4).map((p,i)=><td data-label={spaces[i].name+' '+spaces[i].id}>{pair(p)}</td>)}</tr><tr><th>q</th>{route(example.q).slice(0,4).map((p,i)=><td data-label={spaces[i].name+' '+spaces[i].id}>{pair(p)}</td>)}</tr><tr><th>q − p</th><td data-label="Card O">(10, 0)</td><td data-label="Group G">(10, 0)</td><td data-label="World W">(0, 10)</td><td data-label="View V">(0, 20)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 </Figure>
 <p><strong>Translation</strong> adds an offset: x′ = x + 30 and y′ = y + 20 for O to G. The prime marks the output value. A point receives that offset; a displacement does not. Adding a point in O to a displacement expressed in W would mix directions and units. Convert them to one space first.</p>
 <p><strong>Uniform scale</strong> multiplies both coordinates by the same factor: x′ = 2x, y′ = 2y. It doubles the distance between points too. Thus a ten-unit world step becomes twenty view units at zoom 2. Panning adds a displacement measured in V after that scaling. Neither operation requires rewriting the card’s local points.</p>

 <h2 id="rotation">Turn the group around a known pivot</h2>
 <p>For the group’s clockwise quarter-turn, a unit step along x, (1, 0), becomes (0, 1); a unit step along y, (0, 1), becomes (−1, 0). Any position (x, y) is x steps along the first axis plus y along the second. After turning, it is therefore (−y, x). Review’s G (40, 30) turns to (−30, 40), then the group’s placement adds (160, 100), producing W (130, 140).</p>
 <p>For any angle θ, the turned x unit step is (cos θ, sin θ). <strong>Cosine</strong> and <strong>sine</strong> are its horizontal and vertical components on a circle of radius one. The turned y unit step, still a quarter-turn from it, is (−sin θ, cos θ). Multiplying each by the original number of steps gives the general rule:</p>
 <pre class="equation" aria-label="Rotation equations"><code>x′ = (cos θ)x − (sin θ)y
y′ = (sin θ)x + (cos θ)y</code></pre>
 <div class="circle-inset"><svg viewBox="0 0 280 180" role="img" aria-label="Unit circle with clockwise angle theta; the turned x unit step has components cosine theta and sine theta"><circle cx="80" cy="75" r="50" class="line"/><path class="line" d="M20 75H145 M80 15V135 M80 75L123.3 100 M123.3 75V100"/><path class="line" d="M105 75A25 25 0 0 1 101.65 87.5"/><text x="106" y="94">θ</text><text x="83" y="68">cos θ</text><text x="133" y="97">sin θ</text><text x="28" y="159">Turned unit step: (cos θ, sin θ)</text></svg></div>
 <p>The controls and <code>DOMMatrix.rotate()</code> use degrees: a quarter-turn is 90°. JavaScript’s <code>Math.sin</code> and <code>Math.cos</code> take radians, where a whole turn is 2π, so convert with <code>radians = degrees * Math.PI / 180</code>. At 90°, cosine is zero and sine is one, giving the quarter-turn rule above.</p>
 <p>That rule turns around the space’s origin. A <strong>pivot</strong> is a chosen point that a turn must leave fixed. To turn around the card centre c = (40, 20), first subtract c so the centre becomes the origin, turn, then add c back. Only after that do we apply the card’s (30, 20) placement in G.</p>
 <PivotFigure />
 <p>For the centre itself, the subtraction gives (0, 0), turning keeps (0, 0), and adding c restores it. Turning directly about the origin instead sends centre (40, 20) to (−20, 40), so its G position becomes (10, 60). The pivot calculation explains why a card can appear to orbit when the intention was to spin it in place.</p>
 <h3 id="view-rotation">The view can turn by the same rule</h3>
 <p>Turn the baseline V result around the view centre (400, 300). The marked spot V (320, 320) is displaced (−80, 20) from that centre. A clockwise quarter-turn sends that displacement to (−20, −80), and adding the centre gives V (380, 220). This turns the view of the board; it changes neither the card nor Review lane’s stored placement. The live editor’s <em>Turn view about centre</em> button performs this reset-and-turn operation.</p>

 <h2 id="affine">Six numbers describe the whole placement</h2>
 <p>All these conversions can be written in one form. Map the origin to (e, f), the x unit step to (a, b), and the y unit step to (c, d). To map (x, y), take x copies of the first step and y copies of the second, then add the mapped origin:</p>
 <pre class="equation"><code>x′ = ax + cy + e
y′ = bx + dy + f</code></pre>
 <p>This is a <strong>2D affine transform</strong>: a linear part, the four numbers that map steps, plus a translation. It maps a straight line to a straight line, or to a point if that direction collapses. Parallel directions remain parallel unless collapsed. It need not preserve lengths or angles: scaling x and y differently, or slanting one axis, changes both.</p>
 <Figure number={4} title="Two steps and an origin" caption="The card-to-world mapping has origin (140, 130), x step (0, 1) and y step (−1, 0). The unit-step strokes are enlarged schematically to show their directions; their labels give the actual mapped displacements." text="M_W_from_O has coefficients [0,1,−1,0,140,130]. Its equations are xW=140−yO and yW=130+xO.">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" role="img" aria-label="Card origin at world 140,130, its x step points down and y step left"><path class="ink" d="M110 35V105 M110 35H40"/><circle class="mark" cx="110" cy="35" r="4"/><text x="125" y="35">origin (140, 130)</text><text x="120" y="102">x step (0, 1)</text><text x="10" y="145">y step (−1, 0) ←</text></svg>
 </Figure>
 <p>For this mapping, e and f come from sending the card origin through its placement and the group turn: (0, 0) → (30, 20) → (−20, 30) → (140, 130). The steps turn but receive no translation. Thus a = 0, b = 1, c = −1, d = 0, e = 140, f = 130.</p>
 <p>A <strong>matrix</strong> is a compact table for these equations. Write the point as a column, with an extra 1 at its bottom:</p>
 <pre class="equation" aria-label="Affine matrix times point column"><code>┌x′┐   ┌a c e┐ ┌x┐
│y′│ = │b d f│ │y│
└1 ┘   └0 0 1┘ └1┘</code></pre>
 <p>Each output is one row multiplied by that column and added up. The extra 1 makes the translation appear as e × 1 and f × 1. Give a displacement an extra <strong>0</strong> instead, and the same table omits translation. This extra-coordinate form is called <strong>homogeneous coordinates</strong>; here it simply lets one operation handle both positions and steps.</p>
 <p>The browser’s six-value order is <code>[a, b, c, d, e, f]</code>, so the first two entries are the x step, the next two the y step, and the last two the origin. It is different from reading the displayed rows left to right. To leave every point unchanged, keep unit steps (1, 0) and (0, 1) and origin (0, 0): <code>[1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0]</code>. That is the <strong>identity</strong> mapping.</p>

 <h2 id="composition">Put the conversions together in the right order</h2>
 <p>A card-to-group conversion followed by a group-to-world conversion gives one card-to-world conversion. Combining them this way is <strong>composition</strong>. You already did it by substitution: x_G = x_O + 30, y_G = y_O + 20; then x_W = 160 − y_G and y_W = 100 + x_G. Substituting gives x_W = 140 − y_O and y_W = 130 + x_O, the six numbers above.</p>
 <p>Matrix multiplication packages exactly this substitution. If A acts after B, the combined x coefficient for x is <code>a_A a_B + c_A b_B</code>, because x from B can contribute through both A’s x and y inputs. The combined x translation is <code>a_A e_B + c_A f_B + e_A</code>. For group after card, that is 0 × 30 + (−1) × 20 + 160 = 140. The other coefficients follow the same substitution.</p>
 <p>Name each matrix’s destination and source: <code>M_W_from_G</code> converts G into W. Then the full route reads:</p>
 <pre class="equation"><code>M_C_from_O = M_C_from_V M_V_from_W M_W_from_G M_G_from_O</code></pre>
 <p>Adjacent space names match, so the output of each conversion fits the next. We use column vectors; the rightmost factor acts on the point first. Abbreviate translation as T, scale as S and rotation as R. Then the view is T(60, 40) S(2): scale world coordinates first, add the V pan afterwards. The pivot construction is T(c) R(θ) T(−c), in the same order that subtract, turn and add require.</p>
 <Figure number={5} title="Follow the card" caption="The stepwise route and the composed route agree. The last column uses the browser’s six-value order [a, b, c, d, e, f]. This table uses the fixed full-size bridge snapshot." text="The route is O (10,10), G (40,30), W (130,140), V (320,320), C (440,400). Composed O-to-C coefficients are [0,2,−2,0,460,380].">
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Forward coordinate route and composed matrices"><table class="coordinate-data route-table"><thead><tr><th>Destination</th><th>Marked point</th><th>Composed from O</th></tr></thead><tbody>{spaces.map((s,i)=><tr><th>{s.name} {s.id}</th><td data-label="Marked point">{pair(route()[i])}</td><td data-label="Composed from O"><code>{[[1,0,0,1,0,0],[1,0,0,1,30,20],cardWorld,cardView,cardClient][i].join(', ')}</code></td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
 </Figure>
 <p>Order matters. If you write S(2) T(60, 40), the view translation is applied in world units and then doubled too. The marked point becomes 2 × ((130, 140) + (60, 40)) = (380, 360), rather than 2 × (130, 140) + (60, 40) = (320, 320). Changing zoom now also changes the visible pan distance.</p>
 <OrderFigure />
 <p>The first panel models a pan stored in V. Use it when dragging the whole board should move it a chosen distance in the editor’s view, independent of zoom. A translation inside the scale can be useful too, but it is then expressed in the earlier space. A transform order is a decision about what the parameters mean.</p>

 <h2 id="inverse">Follow the pointer back to the card</h2>
 <p>An <strong>inverse</strong> undoes a conversion. To reverse the full route, undo the last step first: remove the browser bridge, undo view pan and zoom, undo the group placement and turn, then remove the card placement.</p>
 <Figure number={7} title="Out and back" caption="Reverse the baseline client point by undoing one operation at a time. The return reaches the original local point; it does not create a second card position. Illustrative arithmetic." text="C(440,400) minus bridge(120,80) gives V(320,320). Subtract pan(60,40) and divide by 2 to get W(130,140). Subtract group origin(160,100), undo the turn, then subtract card translation(30,20) to get O(10,10).">
  <ol class="return-route"><li><strong>C (440, 400)</strong> − (120, 80) → V (320, 320)</li><li><strong>V (320, 320)</strong> − (60, 40), then ÷ 2 → W (130, 140)</li><li><strong>W (130, 140)</strong> − (160, 100) → (−30, 40); undo turn → G (40, 30)</li><li><strong>G (40, 30)</strong> − (30, 20) → O (10, 10)</li></ol>
 </Figure>
 <p>For a general affine mapping, subtract its translation first: u = x′ − e and v = y′ − f. You need to solve u = ax + cy and v = bx + dy. Multiply the first equation by d and the second by c, then subtract to eliminate y: du − cv = (ad − bc)x. Eliminating x similarly gives av − bu = (ad − bc)y. Therefore:</p>
 <pre class="equation"><code>x = (du − cv) / (ad − bc)
y = (av − bu) / (ad − bc)</code></pre>
 <p>The shared denominator, <strong>ad − bc</strong>, is the <strong>determinant</strong>. It must be non-zero before you divide. For our O-to-C mapping, x_C = 460 − 2y_O and y_C = 380 + 2x_O, so the inverse is x_O = (y_C − 380) / 2 and y_O = (460 − x_C) / 2. Client (440, 400) recovers O (10, 10).</p>
 <p>The inverse of a product reverses its factors for the same reason you remove a coat before the shirt underneath it. Undo the browser bridge before undoing the view, and so on. In code, you can instead compose the forward mapping once and ask for its inverse. The result is the same.</p>
 <p>Once the pointer is in O, the <strong>hit test</strong>, the test of whether it falls in the shape, is just <code>0 ≤ x ≤ 80</code> and <code>0 ≤ y ≤ 40</code>. This tests Review’s filled rectangle, including its boundary. Stroke width, overlapping cards, occlusion and which object wins selection require separate policies.</p>

 <h3 id="browser">Connect the maths to browser coordinates</h3>
 <p>SVG gives each nested <code>&lt;g&gt;</code>, a group of drawing elements, its own transformed coordinates. The outer group below maps W to V, Review lane maps G to W, and the card maps O to G. Its rectangle can keep its simple local dimensions.</p>
 <Code code={nested} lang="html" theme="css-variables" themes={themes} />
 <p>Construct the same matrices in JavaScript. <code>multiply(other)</code> returns this matrix times the other matrix, so <code>worldToView.multiply(groupToWorld)</code> applies the group mapping first. The methods here return new matrices; angles are in degrees.</p>
 <Code code={matrixCode} lang="javascript" theme="css-variables" themes={themes} />
 <p>Apply the three separate placements to their corresponding SVG groups. The SVG tree then composes them. Applying <code>cardToView</code> to the card while keeping its transformed ancestors would apply those ancestors twice.</p>
 <Code code={applyCode} lang="javascript" theme="css-variables" themes={themes} />
 <p>A pointer’s <code>clientX</code> and <code>clientY</code> are in C. Ask the card element for <code>getScreenCTM()</code>, its current local-to-client matrix, then invert it once. Despite “screen” in the method’s name, this mapping ends in browser client coordinates. It includes the ancestor SVG placements and the browser bridge. Do not apply our explicit model chain again, add page scroll, or multiply the pointer by device pixel ratio.</p>
 <Code code={guarded} lang="javascript" theme="css-variables" themes={themes} />
 <p>The explicit copy into <code>DOMMatrix</code> gives the guard a consistent matrix interface, while rejecting an explicitly 3D return first. The remaining checks reject a missing matrix and any inverse or result containing a non-finite number such as <code>NaN</code> or infinity. A failed conversion leaves the last valid interaction state intact. The next section explains the geometric reason an inverse may fail.</p>
 <BrowserFigure />
 <p><em>Check current mapping</em> is a useful debugging comparison: it obtains the browser bridge from the root SVG, composes bridge × view × group × card, and compares the coefficients with the card’s browser-supplied matrix. It also sends the mark out to actual client coordinates and back. Small floating-point differences are expected. A large mismatch would point to a rendering transform that the stored model did not include.</p>
 <p><a href={editorUrl} download="review-editor.html">Download the complete small editor</a> to open a self-contained HTML example. It includes the nested SVG, DOMMatrix rendering, pan/zoom/angle controls, the chosen-centre turn, anchored zoom at the mark and the guarded rectangle test. The displayed angle control turns about the world origin, whose view position is the pan; the centre-turn button uses the different pivot derived above.</p>
 <Figure number={9} title="Half-size editor" caption="Keep viewBox 0 0 800 600, but render it at 400 by 300 CSS pixels. Matching aspect ratios make the bridge a half-scale followed by the same client offset (120, 80). These are model snapshots, not the live figure’s current layout." text="At full size, V(320,320) reaches C(440,400). At half size it reaches C(280,240). Subtracting the SVG’s upper-left gives (160,160), which still needs dividing by .5 to recover V(320,320).">
  <div class="panels"><div class="panel"><p class="panel-label">800 × 600 CSS pixels</p><p>V (320, 320) × 1 + (120, 80)</p><p><strong>C (440, 400)</strong></p></div><div class="panel"><p class="panel-label">400 × 300 CSS pixels</p><p>V (320, 320) × 0.5 + (120, 80)</p><p><strong>C (280, 240)</strong></p></div></div>
 </Figure>
 <p>Subtracting the SVG’s bounding rectangle removes its offset, but leaves CSS-pixel coordinates. At half size, that produces (160, 160), not V (320, 320). With a different aspect ratio, SVG fitting may also add empty margins; ancestor transforms can change axes. The browser-supplied matrix accounts for the current bridge. Read it from the appropriate element when handling input, after committing the transform state used for drawing.</p>

 <h2 id="collapse">When the way back disappears</h2>
 <p>The determinant also tells you what happened to area. The mapped unit steps (a, b) and (c, d) form two sides of a parallelogram. Its signed area is ad − bc. Zero means those sides have become collinear, or one has vanished: the mapping has flattened a two-dimensional region into a line or point. There is no longer enough information to recover both original coordinates.</p>
 <Figure number={10} title="Collapsed group" caption="Set group horizontal scale to zero before its quarter-turn. This is a mathematical trace: SVG may suppress painting with a singular transform, so this figure does not attempt to render that transform as a collapsed card." text="Distinct O points p(10,10) and q(20,10) both reach W(130,100), V(320,240) and C(440,320). The determinant is zero, so one client point cannot identify which original point was used.">
  <div class="panels">{[example.p,example.q].map((p,i)=><div class="panel"><p class="panel-label">{i===0?'p':'q'} in O {pair(p)}</p><p>↓ card placement, zero horizontal group scale, turn, place, view, bridge</p><p><strong>C {pair(point(collapsedClient,p))}</strong></p></div>)}</div>
 </Figure>
 <p>Both points had the same y in G. Zero horizontal scale erased their different x values before the turn. An inverse cannot choose between them; an API cannot reconstruct information that the forward mapping discarded.</p>
 <p>A negative determinant is different. For example, reversing x while keeping y gives determinant −1: an axis is reflected, but distinct points remain distinct and the mapping can still be inverted. A tiny non-zero scale is invertible in mathematics yet may magnify pointer error badly. If one world unit becomes a millionth of a client pixel in one direction, a one-pixel pointer error can become a million world units on the return.</p>
 <p>This editor accepts finite state, limits its positive zoom to 0.25–4, rejects failed or non-finite conversions and retains the last valid interaction state. Those zoom limits are an editor policy. A universal test such as “absolute determinant greater than a small constant” would not establish numerical stability: determinant measures area, and a severe squeeze on one axis can be hidden by a large stretch on the other. Allowing arbitrary scale and shear needs error limits suited to the application.</p>

 <h2 id="anchors">Keep the chosen point under the pointer</h2>
 <p>The coordinate route lets you derive interactions from a condition they keep true. Such a condition is an <strong>invariant</strong>. Panning changes the view translation in V. Turning changes a specified placement about a named pivot. Moving Review inside its lane changes the card translation in G. Choosing the correct stored parameter is half the implementation.</p>
 <p>For anchored zoom, keep the same world point under a chosen view position. At baseline, world anchor a_W = (130, 140) is at view target a_V = (320, 320). After changing zoom from 2 to 3, solve a_V = 3a_W + newPan. Thus newPan = (320, 320) − (390, 420) = (−70, −100). Leaving pan at (60, 40) would move the anchor to (450, 460), away from the pointer.</p>
 <p>For a turned view, first turn the world anchor, then scale it: <code>newPan = a_V − newZoom × R(newAngle) a_W</code>. All quantities being subtracted are now in V. Obtain a_W from the old view inverse and a_V from the current client-to-V conversion. Use the actual bounded zoom when solving, so reaching a zoom limit does not introduce drift.</p>
 <p>A card drag keeps the particular point you grabbed in O under the moving pointer. Save that local point at pointer-down. At each update, map the pointer into G, the card’s parent space, and subtract the saved local point: <code>newCardTranslation_G = pointer_G − grabbedPoint_O</code>. This subtraction works here because the card placement is translation-only: its O steps and G steps have the same axes and units. With a fixed card rotation or scale L, subtract <code>L × grabbedPoint_O</code> instead, including any fixed pivot offset consistently.</p>
 <p>In the baseline drag, client (440, 400) → (480, 420) is movement (40, 20) in C and V, (20, 10) in W, and (10, −20) in G because the group is turned. Add that G displacement to the card’s starting (30, 20), giving (40, 0). Dividing the client movement by zoom alone gives a W displacement; adding it straight to the G placement changes the wrong axes. A resized SVG adds a unit conversion too.</p>
 <p>Build the editor by keeping each geometry local, naming every conversion’s source and destination, composing in the order the point travels, and stating each rotation’s pivot. Render from one current state, then read the appropriate current browser mapping for input. Invert with guards before changing stored state, and check a candidate update by mapping the retained point forward again.</p>
 <p>That is enough to implement the geometric core. <a href="/review/skill-transfer/gestures/">Keep a diagram under the pointer</a> develops these invariants into one small gesture implementation: pan, zoom, chosen-pivot view rotation and card drag, including resized and turned views. It also handles pointer capture, completion and cancellation, where correct geometry alone cannot keep an interrupted gesture working.</p>

 <section class="essay-sources" aria-labelledby="sources"><h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
 <p>Checked on 21 October 2026. The scalar derivations and worked snapshots are this article’s illustrative model. The live figure exercises browser SVG/DOMMatrix mapping; it is not a claim about every browser or input device. Perspective, rendering engines and diagram layout are outside this 2D affine example.</p>
 <ul><li>W3C, <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/SVG2/coords.html">SVG 2: Coordinate Systems, Transformations and Units</a>: user spaces, viewBox fitting and the initial axes.</li><li>W3C, <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/SVG2/types.html#InterfaceSVGGraphicsElement">SVGGraphicsElement</a>: the mapping returned by <code>getScreenCTM()</code>, including ancestor transforms and the browser bridge.</li><li>W3C, <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/geometry-1/">Geometry Interfaces Module Level 1</a>: six-value DOMMatrix order, multiplication, degrees, DOMPoint transformation and non-invertible matrices.</li><li>W3C, <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#extensions-to-the-mouseevent-interface">CSSOM View: MouseEvent coordinates</a>: client coordinates relative to the viewport and their distinction from page coordinates.</li><li><a href="/review/skill-transfer/gestures/">Keep a diagram under the pointer</a>: the companion implementation of gesture invariants and event lifetime.</li></ul></section>
